  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n\n- Near-normal temperatures with the weather pattern during this \n  week, along with mostly dry conditions.\n\n- Increasing winds Tuesday, especially across northwest Montana and\n  the higher terrain. \n\n- Scattered rain showers Tuesday night into early Wednesday then \n  slight warming trend into the remainder of the week.\n\n- Confidence increasing for a warmer and drier than normal early \n  October beyond the 7 day forecast. \n\n\nWidespread high/mid level cloud cover and a few light showers will\ngradually diminish this morning by sunrise as a weak upper level trough\npasses through. Then mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies will \npromote solar heating and near- normal afternoon temperatures. \nWest winds will become locally breezy during the afternoon across \nnorthwest Montana, generally remaining below 20 mph.\n\nBy Tuesday, a decaying moisture plume from British Columbia and \nnorthwest Washington will reach northwest Montana. Expect \nincreasing cloud cover along with gusty west-to-southwest winds \nranging from 10 to 20 mph, with peak gusts around 30 mph. High-\nresolution guidance highlights a 60% probability of gusts \nexceeding 30 mph along the US-2 corridor between Happy's Inn and \nKalispell. Across Flathead Lake, sustained winds near 20 mph are \nonly expected in a few places such as around Wild Horse Island, \ncentral lake zones, and southern Polson Bay, producing choppy \nwater conditions.\n\nThe primary moisture axis shifts over northwest Montana late \nTuesday night through Wednesday morning, focusing light rainfall \nmainly over mountain terrain. Valleys extending from Lowell, \nIdaho, northeastward through the Seeley-Swan Valley to Glacier \nNational Park have a 40% to 60% chance of receiving measurable \nprecipitation (0.01 inches or greater) by Wednesday morning.\n\nModel consensus points to a stable zonal flow pattern late in the\nweek, bringing a slight warming trend, mostly dry conditions, and\nroutine afternoon breezes. \n\nNot much has changed from the previous in-depth discussion on\nearly October; it continues to look warmer and drier than normal.\nHere are the details once again: Confidence is increasing for a \nwarm, dry early October beyond the 7 day forecast. The large scale\nweather pattern is being influenced by upstream tropical \nactivity. With 2 hurricanes and 1 tropical cyclone in the central \nand east Pacific Ocean from Hawaii to Baja and a typhoon \napproaching the eastern periphery of Japan, it's been a difficult \nforecast for models. However, they're starting to coalesce around \nhigher chances for a potent ridge over the northern Rockies into \nthe first third of October. Ensemble mean temperature forecasts \nare coming in 15 degrees above seasonal averages at about 9-12 \ndays out. Those are large anomalies for that long of a lead time! \nAdd in that the signal is coming from multiple ensemble systems \nincluding both dynamical (physics based) and AI modeling systems, \nthis provides above normal levels of confidence for such a long \nlead time forecast. Looking back, the last time this kind of warm \nridge set up over the northwestern US in early October was october\n9-11 and 14, 1991. Eureka, Montana hit 86 degrees, Grangeville, \nIdaho 87 degrees, and Butte 81 degrees! The Center for Climate \nPrediction has issued forecasts for the Northern Rockies region \nwith a 80-90% chance of above average temperatures and \nprecipitation forecasts are leaning below average for that time \nperiod as well.",
